WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.786
The sphere in front of you is the Moon.

00:00:02.786 --> 00:00:06.573
Every yellow marker is a place a spacecraft has been.

00:00:06.573 --> 00:00:07.645
Drag to rotate.

00:00:07.645 --> 00:00:09.574
Zoom in to read the labels.

00:00:09.574 --> 00:00:11.717
Some of those markers are old.

00:00:11.717 --> 00:00:16.076
Luna 9, in 1966, was the first soft landing on another world.

00:00:16.076 --> 00:00:22.720
Six Apollo crewed landings, between 1969 and 1972, sit in a cluster on the visible near side.

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:24.006
Tranquillity Base.

00:00:24.006 --> 00:00:25.149
Ocean of Storms.

00:00:25.149 --> 00:00:25.864
Fra Mauro.

00:00:25.864 --> 00:00:26.793
Hadley Rille.

00:00:26.793 --> 00:00:27.507
Descartes.

00:00:27.507 --> 00:00:28.579
Taurus-Littrow.

00:00:28.579 --> 00:00:30.079
Some markers are new.

00:00:30.079 --> 00:00:42.368
Chang'e 4 landed on the lunar far side in 2019 — the first soft landing there in human history, and the first to require a relay satellite, Queqiao, to get its signal home.

00:00:42.368 --> 00:00:54.586
Chandrayaan-3 placed the Vikram lander near the south pole in 2023 — the first soft landing in that part of the Moon, where craters in permanent shadow may hold water ice.

00:00:54.586 --> 00:00:55.800
Click any marker.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:06.803
The right panel opens with the mission's record — operating agency, lander type, crew if any, scientific instruments, surface duration, gallery of images.

00:01:06.803 --> 00:01:18.163
Most of the Apollo missions carry the lunar module model — descent stage on the surface, ascent stage either present or already gone, depending on the mission.

00:01:18.163 --> 00:01:29.309
The robotic missions carry their own per-mission three-D models — Luna 9's petal-capsule, Lunokhod 1's bathtub-on-wheels, Yutu-2's solar-paneled silhouette.

00:01:29.309 --> 00:01:32.096
Look at the cluster around the equator.

00:01:32.096 --> 00:01:45.599
That's where the Apollo program landed because the trajectories were cheapest there — direct ascents from Cape Kennedy, free-return geometry, navigation simplest near zero degrees latitude.

00:01:45.599 --> 00:01:47.242
Look at the south pole.

00:01:47.242 --> 00:01:49.743
That's where Artemis is going next.

00:01:49.743 --> 00:02:00.460
Different geometry, different fuel cost, different scientific motivation — water ice, permanently shadowed craters, the long-term lunar base argument.

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:09.391
Use the filter chips at the top to show only crewed landings, only lander-rovers, only sample-return missions, only failures.

00:02:09.391 --> 00:02:15.178
Cross-check the missions you remember against the missions you didn't know about.

00:02:15.178 --> 00:02:18.179
Twelve people have walked on this surface.

00:02:18.179 --> 00:02:23.180
Eleven robotic landers have made it down softly since the last Apollo.

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:37.327
At this moment, three rovers — Yutu-2, the Chandrayaan-3 Pragyan rover, and another mission whose name will appear here once it lands — are or recently were operating on the surface in front of you.

00:02:37.327 --> 00:02:38.470
Look at the map.

00:02:38.470 --> 00:02:41.257
See how recent most of the activity is.

